---
import SiteLayout from "../../layouts/SiteLayout.astro";

const basePath = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, "");
---

<SiteLayout
  title="Privacy and security"
  description="What stays on your machine, what leaves for your model provider, and the current safety boundaries in Veasel Code."
>
  <section class="doc-page page-grid">
    <p class="kicker">TRUST / DATA FLOW</p>
    <h1>
      Know what moves.
      <br />
      <em>Keep the reins.</em>
    </h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Veasel Code runs its server locally and stores session history in SQLite.
      Model prompts are sent to the provider configured by the person running
      the server. Local storage does not mean local inference.
    </p>
    <div class="data-flow" aria-label="Current message data flow">
      <div>
        <small>YOU</small>
        <strong>Terminal client</strong>
        <span>loopback HTTP</span>
      </div>
      <b aria-hidden="true">→</b>
      <div>
        <small>LOCAL MACHINE</small>
        <strong>V server + SQLite</strong>
        <span>session history</span>
      </div>
      <b aria-hidden="true">→</b>
      <div>
        <small>YOUR CONFIGURED PROVIDER</small>
        <strong>Model API</strong>
        <span>prompt and recent context</span>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="doc-panel">
      <h2>Current protections</h2>
      <ul class="guide-list">
        <li>
          The backend binds to loopback; remote server binding is not
          implemented.
        </li>
        <li>
          Provider credentials stay in the backend environment and are not
          returned by the API.
        </li>
        <li>
          Remote model endpoints require HTTPS; local development endpoints may
          use loopback HTTP.
        </li>
        <li>
          Provider redirects are rejected, and response sizes and message counts
          are bounded.
        </li>
        <li>
          New sessions accept an existing workspace directory and store its
          canonical path.
        </li>
        <li>
          Workspace listing, file reads, and literal search stay inside that
          root, skip symlinks during traversal, and enforce file, byte, and
          result limits.
        </li>
        <li>
          Provider tool calls are bounded by round, call, argument, and result
          limits. The model can propose a bounded replacement for one text file;
          the normal TUI flow opens the saved diff before explicit approval and
          writing.
        </li>
        <li>
          Shell commands are stored for review. The TUI shows the exact command,
          working directory, and timeout before approval; execution is bounded
          to 300 seconds and 64 KiB of captured output. Commands inherit the
          server user's environment and permissions and have no OS sandbox.
        </li>
        <li>
          Agent Plugins support is experimental. MCP servers require per-session
          trust, and every model-requested MCP tool call requires separate
          inspection and one-time approval before it reaches the server. A
          rejection is not retried. Stdio processes inherit the server user's
          permissions and have no OS sandbox. Streamable HTTP refuses redirects
          and validates TLS, but configured headers and approved tool inputs are
          sent to the chosen server origin.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="doc-panel">
      <h2>What to keep in mind</h2>
      <ul class="guide-list">
        <li>
          Your model provider receives the prompt and conversation history
          needed to answer.
        </li>
        <li>
          The loopback API has no application token; other programs running as
          your user may reach it, read files beneath an active session root, and
          call the diff and approval routes. TUI review is a workflow, not an
          enforced caller-identity boundary.
        </li>
        <li>
          The model may request reads from the selected workspace or propose an
          edit. Returned excerpts and tool results are sent to your configured
          provider. These tools do not provide a filesystem sandbox against
          other processes running as your user.
        </li>
        <li>
          Enabled Skill instructions, MCP tool arguments and results, and
          approved shell output may be sent to your configured model provider.
        </li>
        <li>
          The official mascot artwork carries a noncommercial license;
          commercial distribution needs separate permission.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <aside class="callout">
      <strong>Review before a file changes.</strong> Repository content and tool
      output are treated as untrusted data. A proposal alone does not write to
      disk; a recorded diff view, explicit approval, and a matching file
      snapshot are required. The unauthenticated loopback API allows same-user
      processes to call those routes. Shell commands are reviewed before
      execution but are not sandboxed. MCP calls are held before execution until
      their exact arguments are inspected and approved; the unauthenticated
      loopback API means same-user processes can also access those review and
      approval routes. Trusted stdio servers still run without an OS sandbox.
    </aside>
    <p class="doc-next">
      <a
        class="text-link"
        href="https://github.com/veasel-labs/veasel/blob/main/docs/security/threat-model.md"
      >
        Read the threat model <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span>
      </a>
      <a class="text-link" href={`${basePath}/docs/plugins/`}>
        Explore plugin safety <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
      </a>
    </p>
  </section>
</SiteLayout>
